r for conducting the ASR task.\nWe pick TinyLLaMA of the 1B-magnitude and LLaMA-2 of the 7B-magnitude to make a preliminary assessment.\n TinyLLaMA \n LLaMA-2 \n TinyLLaMA-Chat \n LLaMA-2-Chat \n test-clean \n test-other \n test-clean \n test-other \n test-clean \n test-other \n test-clean \n test-other \n 16.16 \n 9.55 \n 8.97 \n 18.77 \n 7.35 \n 17.46 \n 7.03 \n 6.37 \n 6.61 \n 6.41 \n 8.94 \n 8.95 \n 6.10 \n 8.67 \n 6.37 \n 8.22 \n 7.15 \n 8.62 \n 6.79 \nAs shown in Table  3 , the Phi-2 chat model with 2.78B parameters has a comparable word error rate with LLaMA-2 with 6.74B parameters on test-other.\nVicuna is an open-source chat LLM fine-tuned on user-shared conversational data collected from ShareGPT 13 13 13 https://sharegpt.com , utilizing LLaMA as a pre-trained LLM.\nThe LLM-based ASR model shows better results when Vicuna is used as the LLM compared with LLaMA-2 and LLaMA-2-Chat.\n test-clean \n test-other \n TinyLLaMA \n 17.31M \n 8.22 \n LLaMA-2 \n 6.74B \n 7.15 \n TinyLLaMA-Chat \n 17.31M \n 8.62 \n 18.35M \n 7.19 \n LLaMA-2-Chat \n 6.74B \n 6.79 \n Vicuna \n 6.74B \n 6.47 \n Table 4: Explore the performance with different speech encoders for LLM-based ASR. The projector is fixed with linear layers and LLM is fixed with Vicuna-7B-v1.5. LS-960 means the Librispeech 960 hours dataset. \n test-clean \n test-other \n 68.95 \n 99.37 \n 7.63M \n 7.07 \n 16.01 \n 19.82M \n 87.00M \n 16.26M \n 9.50 \n 18.88M \n 6.79 \n 6.47 \n 6.35 \n HuBERT Base \n 16.26M \n WavLM Base", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785315837882", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-a1a496cd29e2b7e1", "name": "Bash", "input": {"command": "grep -iE \"WavLM.*Large|WavLM.*Base|6\\.47|6\\.35|6\\.79|9\\.50|7\\.07|SLAM.ASR|our.*model|proposed|best|state.of.the.art|SOTA\" /tmp/slam_asr.txt | head -40", "description": "Extract SLAM-ASR best WER numbers"}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "38f8b140-304d-4f8f-ac56-b2fdbafebccb", "uuid": "61155196-b683-45be-ae31-af348518ea12"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a1a496cd29e2b7e1", "type": "tool_result", "content": " 2.3 Proposed Method \n 4.4 SLAM-ASR \n Authors: achieve the best HTML results from your LaTeX submissions by following these best practices . \nTo be more specific, we benchmark and explore various combinations of LLMs and speech encoders, leading to the optimal LLM-based ASR system, which we call SLAM-ASR 1 1 1 SLAM-ASR is a subproject of SLAM-LLM, where SLAM stands for S peech, L anguage, A udio and M usic. Working in progress and will open-source soon. .\nThe proposed SLAM-ASR provides a clean setup and little task-specific design, where only the linear projector is trained.\nTo the best of our knowledge, SLAM-ASR achieves the best performance on the Librispeech benchmark among LLM-based ASR models and even outperforms the latest LLM-based audio-universal model trained on massive pair data.\nBuilding upon these insights, we propose SLAM-ASR, in which only a linear projector is trained to conduct the ASR task. SLAM-ASR only requires 4 4 4 4 GPUs for 4 4 4 4 hours of training to achieve state-of-the-art performance on the Librispeech  Panayotov et al. ( 2015 ) corpus, compared with other LLM-based ASR models and a series of previous best performing NN-based ASR models.\nBesides, our work embarks on an in-depth exploration of the ability of LLM-based ASR models. Interestingly,\n SLAM-ASR \n 2.3 Proposed Method \n Figure 1: A brief pipeline of SLAM-ASR, at the core of which is a frozen speech encoder and a frozen LLM, with the only trainable linear projector to align between speech and text modalities. \n As shown in Figure  1 , an embarrassingly simple framework is proposed to train the SLAM-ASR model.\nFor the base-size models, both HuBERT  Hsu et al. ( 2021 ) and WavLM  Chen et al. ( 2022 ) perform self-supervised pre-training on LibriSpeech  Panayotov et al. ( 2015 ) corpus with 960 960 960 960 hours. For the large-size models, HuBERT is trained on LibriLight  Kahn et al. ( 2020 ) corpus with 60 , 000 60 000 60,000 60 , 000 hours, while WavLM is trained on the much larger 94 , 000 94 000 94,000 94 , 000 hours data including LibriLight  Kahn et al. ( 2020 ) , VoxPopuli  Wang et al. ( 2021 ) , and GigaSpeech  Chen et al. ( 2021 ) . Furthermore, HuBERT provides pre-trained models of X-Large size, which is the largest publicly available self-supervised speech encoder.\nIn all our experiments, the output of the speech encoder is 50 50 50 50 Hz, and the downsampling rate k = 5 𝑘 5 k=5 italic_k = 5 , leading to the input speech features 𝐄 𝐒 superscript 𝐄 𝐒 \\mathbf{E^{S}} bold_E start_POSTSUPERSCRIPT bold_S end_POSTSUPERSCRIPT of the large model being 10 10 10 10 Hz.\nFinally, we propose SLAM-ASR , and compare SLAM-ASR with state-of-the-art previous NN-based ASR models and the latest best-performing LLM-based ASR models. \n 6.79 \n 6.79 \n 6.47 \n 7.07 \n 9.50 \n 6.79 \n 6.47 \n 6.35 \n WavLM Base \n WavLM Large \n  + LS-960 Fine-tuning (SLAM-ASR) \n \\hdashline SLAM-ASR \n For the self-supervised learning speech encoders, HuBERT Base and WavLM Base have about 95M parameters, with 768 dimensions of hidden size.\nWhen scaling the self-supervised speech encoders to 0.3B, WavLM Large outperforms all listed supervised speech encoders, including Whisper-medium with 0.3B parameters and Whisper-large with 0.6B parameters, while the improvement from HuBERT Base to HuBERT Large is not obvious.\nHowever, if the HuBERT Large encoder is first fine-tuned on Librispeech 960 hours of training data, and used as the speech encoder to train the projector in our LLM-based ASR model, the model achieves a WER of 2.30 % percent 2.30 2.30\\% 2.30 % on test-clean and 4.53 % percent 4.53 4.53\\% 4.53 % on test-other, exceeding the performance with WavLM Large as the speech encoder.\nWith Librispeech-960 fine-tuned HuBERT X-Large, our LLM-based ASR model gets a word error rate of 1.94 % percent 1.94 1.94\\% 1.94 % on test-clean and 3.81 % percent 3.81 3.81\\% 3.81 % on test-other, achieving 24.8 % percent 24.8 24.8\\% 24.8 % and 41.1 % percent 41.1 41.1\\% 41.1 % relative WER reduction over the model with Whisper-large as the speech encoder, respectively.\n SLAM-ASR \n 4.4 SLAM-ASR \n Here we introduce SLAM-ASR, a llm-based ASR model with HuBERT X-Large as the speech encoder and Vicuna-7B as the LLM, with the only trainable linear projector, implemented based on the SLAM-LLM framework.\nCompared with their method, our SLAM-ASR yields 17.4 / 26.9 % 17.4 percent 26.9 17.4/26.9\\% 17.4 / 26.9 % relative WER reductions on test-clean/other subsets trained with the same 960 960 960 960 hours of Librispeech data.\nWhen their model is trained on a larger amount of speech over 4 , 000 4 000 4,000 4 , 000 hours, the proposed SLAM-ASR still performs better.\nWe also compare SLAM-ASR with the latest LLM-based audio-universal models, SALMONN  Tang et al. ( 2024 ) and Qwen-Audio  Chu et al. ( 2023 ) , which provide results on Librispeech benchmark.\nCompared with these audio-based multimodal LLMs, SLAM-ASR still achieves better performance despite the large margin in training data. \n We also compare SLAM-ASR with state-of-the-art previous NN-based models.\nFor specialist models trained on Librispeech-960, we compare SLAM-ASR with ContextNet  Han et al. ( 2020 ) , Conformer Gulati et al. ( 2020 ) , Branchformer  Peng et al. ( 2022 ) , and Zipformer  Yao et al. ( 2024 ) . All models are of large size, and the results from their papers are demonstrated.", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "38f8b140-304d-4f8f-ac56-b2fdbafebccb", "uuid": "87f21e3d-bd6d-4d30-a88c-99a607432b37", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T09:04:33.791Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": " 2.3 Proposed Method \n 4.4 SLAM-ASR \n Authors: achieve the best HTML results from your LaTeX submissions by following these best practices . \nTo be more specific, we benchmark and explore various combinations of LLMs and speech encoders, leading to the optimal LLM-based ASR system, which we call SLAM-ASR 1 1 1 SLAM-ASR is a subproject of SLAM-LLM, where SLAM stands for S peech, L anguage, A udio and M usic. Working in progress and will open-source soon. .\nThe proposed SLAM-ASR provides a clean setup and little task-specific design, where only the linear projector is trained.\nTo the best of our knowledge, SLAM-ASR achieves the best performance on the Librispeech benchmark among LLM-based ASR models and even outperforms the latest LLM-based audio-universal model trained on massive pair data.\nBuilding upon these insights, we propose SLAM-ASR, in which only a linear projector is trained to conduct the ASR task. SLAM-ASR only requires 4 4 4 4 GPUs for 4 4 4 4 hours of training to achieve state-of-the-art performance on the Librispeech  Panayotov et al. ( 2015 ) corpus, compared with other LLM-based ASR models and a series of previous best performing NN-based ASR models.\nBesides, our work embarks on an in-depth exploration of the ability of LLM-based ASR models. Interestingly,\n SLAM-ASR \n 2.3 Proposed Method \n Figure 1: A brief pipeline of SLAM-ASR, at the core of which is a frozen speech encoder and a frozen LLM, with the only trainable linear projector to align between speech and text modalities. \n As shown in Figure  1 , an embarrassingly simple framework is proposed to train the SLAM-ASR model.\nFor the base-size models, both HuBERT  Hsu et al. ( 2021 ) and WavLM  Chen et al. ( 2022 ) perform self-supervised pre-training on LibriSpeech  Panayotov et al. ( 2015 ) corpus with 960 960 960 960 hours. For the large-size models, HuBERT is trained on LibriLight  Kahn et al. ( 2020 ) corpus with 60 , 000 60 000 60,000 60 , 000 hours, while WavLM is trained on the much larger 94 , 000 94 000 94,000 94 , 000 hours data including LibriLight  Kahn et al. ( 2020 ) , VoxPopuli  Wang et al. ( 2021 ) , and GigaSpeech  Chen et al. ( 2021 ) . Furthermore, HuBERT provides pre-trained models of X-Large size, which is the largest publicly available self-supervised speech encoder.\nIn all our experiments, the output of the speech encoder is 50 50 50 50 Hz, and the downsampling rate k = 5 𝑘 5 k=5 italic_k = 5 , leading to the input speech features 𝐄 𝐒 superscript 𝐄 𝐒 \\mathbf{E^{S}} bold_E start_POSTSUPERSCRIPT bold_S end_POSTSUPERSCRIPT of the large model being 10 10 10 10 Hz.\nFinally, we propose SLAM-ASR , and compare SLAM-ASR with state-of-the-art previous NN-based ASR models and the latest best-performing LLM-based ASR models. \n 6.79 \n 6.79 \n 6.47 \n 7.07 \n 9.50 \n 6.79 \n 6.47 \n 6.35 \n WavLM Base \n WavLM Large \n  + LS-960 Fine-tuning (SLAM-ASR) \n \\hdashline SLAM-ASR \n For the self-supervised learning speech encoders, HuBERT Base and WavLM Base have about 95M parameters, with 768 dimensions of hidden size.\nWhen scaling the self-supervised speech encoders to 0.3B, WavLM Large outperforms all listed supervised speech encoders, including Whisper-medium with 0.3B parameters and Whisper-large with 0.6B parameters, while the improvement from HuBERT Base to HuBERT Large is not obvious.\nHowever, if the HuBERT Large encoder is first fine-tuned on Librispeech 960 hours of training data, and used as the speech encoder to train the projector in our LLM-based ASR model, the model achieves a WER of 2.30 % percent 2.30 2.30\\% 2.30 % on test-clean and 4.53 % percent 4.53 4.53\\% 4.53 % on test-other, exceeding the performance with WavLM Large as the speech encoder.\nWith Librispeech-960 fine-tuned HuBERT X-Large, our LLM-based ASR model gets a word error rate of 1.94 % percent 1.94 1.94\\% 1.94 % on test-clean and 3.81 % percent 3.81 3.81\\% 3.81 % on test-other, achieving 24.8 % percent 24.8 24.8\\% 24.8 % and 41.1 % percent 41.1 41.1\\% 41.1 % relative WER reduction over the model with Whisper-large as the speech encoder, respectively.\n SLAM-ASR \n 4.4 SLAM-ASR \n Here we introduce SLAM-ASR, a llm-based ASR model with HuBERT X-Large as the speech encoder and Vicuna-7B as the LLM, with the only trainable linear projector, implemented based on the SLAM-LLM framework.\nCompared with their method, our SLAM-ASR yields 17.4 / 26.9 % 17.4 percent 26.9 17.4/26.9\\% 17.4 / 26.9 % relative WER reductions on test-clean/other subsets trained with the same 960 960 960 960 hours of Librispeech data.\nWhen their model is trained on a larger amount of speech over 4 , 000 4 000 4,000 4 , 000 hours, the proposed SLAM-ASR still performs better.\nWe also compare SLAM-ASR with the latest LLM-based audio-universal models, SALMONN  Tang et al. ( 2024 ) and Qwen-Audio  Chu et al. ( 2023 ) , which provide results on Librispeech benchmark.\nCompared with these audio-based multimodal LLMs, SLAM-ASR still achieves better performance despite the large margin in training data. \n We also compare SLAM-ASR with state-of-the-art previous NN-based models.\nFor specialist models trained on Librispeech-960, we compare SLAM-ASR with ContextNet  Han et al. ( 2020 ) , Conformer Gulati et al. ( 2020 ) , Branchformer  Peng et al. ( 2022 ) , and Zipformer  Yao et al. ( 2024 ) . All models are of large size, and the results from their papers are demonstrated.", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785315899482", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "现在我已经有了清晰的认识。SLAM-ASR 在使用 Vicuna-7B + HuBERT X-Large（在 LS-960 上微调）时，在 LS test-clean 上达到了最佳 WER 1.94%，而 WavLM-Large + Vicuna-7B 的结果大约在 6.35% 左右。本文使用的是 TinyLlama-1.1B（一个更小的 LLM），在 LS-100 上 WER 为 6.1%（中心化），6.4%（联邦）。这是一个完全不同的规模（LS-100 vs LS-960，TinyLlama vs Vicuna-7B）。让我再核查一个事实——Telefonica 的代码仓库，它似乎是一项并发的/非常近期的工作。"}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "38f8b140-304d-4f8f-ac56-b2fdbafebccb", "uuid": "560579d2-6d30-4921-8148-57dfed0bb0b5"}
