{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8608a2a417d872ab", "type": "tool_result", "content": " Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized artificial intelligence by demonstrating remarkable capabilities in language understanding, reasoning, and generation across a wide range of applications [ 14 ] . Their success has recently extended beyond text-only processing to multimodal domains, including speech [ 52 ] . In particular, Speech Large Language Models (SpeechLLMs) integrate acoustic modeling with the powerful contextual modeling abilities of LLMs, enabling unified end-to-end systems for automatic speech recognition (ASR), speech translation, and spoken dialogue. Recent frameworks such as SLAM-ASR [ 26 ] have demonstrated that incorporating LLM-based architectures into speech recognition pipelines can significantly improve robustness, contextual awareness, and generalization compared to traditional encoder-decoder or CTC-based systems [ 46 ] . These approaches move toward a unified modeling paradigm where speech and text are processed within a shared representational space, reducing the need for complex cascaded pipelines and task-specific architectures. \n Future research will extend this framework toward large-scale multilingual and cross-domain federated ASR scenarios involving more diverse acoustic conditions and highly non-IID client distributions. Incorporating advanced communication-efficient techniques such as gradient compression, adaptive client selection, or hierarchical aggregation could further improve scalability. Additionally, integrating differential privacy mechanisms would provide formal privacy guarantees beyond data locality. Exploring personalization strategies, including client-specific adapters or federated continual learning, may help mitigate heterogeneity and close the remaining centralized federated performance gap. Finally, scaling to larger LLM backbones and evaluating real-world on-device deployment constraints will be essential for practical federated SpeechLLM applications. \n SLAM-llm: a modular, open-source multimodal large language model framework and best practice for speech, language, audio and music processing .", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "38f8b140-304d-4f8f-ac56-b2fdbafebccb", "uuid": "b52207c9-c95d-4211-a530-c91d7db35870", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T09:06:43.102Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": " Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized artificial intelligence by demonstrating remarkable capabilities in language understanding, reasoning, and generation across a wide range of applications [ 14 ] . Their success has recently extended beyond text-only processing to multimodal domains, including speech [ 52 ] . In particular, Speech Large Language Models (SpeechLLMs) integrate acoustic modeling with the powerful contextual modeling abilities of LLMs, enabling unified end-to-end systems for automatic speech recognition (ASR), speech translation, and spoken dialogue. Recent frameworks such as SLAM-ASR [ 26 ] have demonstrated that incorporating LLM-based architectures into speech recognition pipelines can significantly improve robustness, contextual awareness, and generalization compared to traditional encoder-decoder or CTC-based systems [ 46 ] . These approaches move toward a unified modeling paradigm where speech and text are processed within a shared representational space, reducing the need for complex cascaded pipelines and task-specific architectures. \n Future research will extend this framework toward large-scale multilingual and cross-domain federated ASR scenarios involving more diverse acoustic conditions and highly non-IID client distributions. Incorporating advanced communication-efficient techniques such as gradient compression, adaptive client selection, or hierarchical aggregation could further improve scalability. Additionally, integrating differential privacy mechanisms would provide formal privacy guarantees beyond data locality. Exploring personalization strategies, including client-specific adapters or federated continual learning, may help mitigate heterogeneity and close the remaining centralized federated performance gap. Finally, scaling to larger LLM backbones and evaluating real-world on-device deployment constraints will be essential for practical federated SpeechLLM applications. \n SLAM-llm: a modular, open-source multimodal large language model framework and best practice for speech, language, audio and music processing .", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785316030407", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "本论文引用了 SLAM-ASR [26]（这是其 SpeechLLM 架构的基础）。它提到了差分隐私（differential privacy）作为未来工作，但并未引用或与以下内容进行比较：\n1. NeurIPS 2025 FL+DP for ASR 论文\n2. ICLR 2024 Apple FL+DP for ASR 论文\n3. Telefonica 并发的 FL+SpeechLLM 代码/工作\n4. 该作者此前的 EFL-PEFT ICASSP 2025 工作（引用为 [3]，用作基准 EL-adapters，但未作为直接的联邦学习框架先驱进行讨论）\n\n让我再做一次关键检查——该作者此前的 EFL-PEFT 工作与本论文的关系有多紧密，以及 Adaptive FedAvg（仅带有 LR 衰减的 FedAvg）是否真的是一个新贡献。"}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "38f8b140-304d-4f8f-ac56-b2fdbafebccb", "uuid": "81564354-3f85-4d9c-b7a1-f57a3f7a2740"}
